  "verbalization": "Centratherin is implicated in the biological pathway leading to ME-180 through two distinct but interconnected mechanisms. The first pathway involves Centratherin directly targeting SN12K1, which in turn is a target of [(1S,4S,6S,7S,9E,12R)-4,9,12-trimethyl-14-oxo-15-propan-2-ylidene-5-oxatricyclo[10.3.0.04,6]pentadec-9-en-7-yl] acetate, a compound that ultimately targets ME-180. The second pathway shows that Centratherin also targets SN12K1, which is a target of Methyl tetradecenoate, which also targets ME-180. Both pathways converge on ME-180 through SN12K1, suggesting that Centratherin may act through a shared mechanism involving SN12K1. The evidence supports a potential role for Centratherin in modulating ME-180 through a common target, SN12K1, highlighting a possible functional overlap in the biological processes governed by these compounds.",
